WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.986 --> 00:00:03.944
[BIRDS CHIRPING]

00:00:06.409 --> 00:00:09.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.980
RONALD REAGAN:
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.380
students of Hillsdale, the
friends of the College,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.170
I'm delighted to be here
the first time, although I

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.140
have been well acquainted for
a long time with what Hillsdale

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:26.640
means.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:32.330
DAVID M. WHALEN: You are
men and women of Hillsdale,

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.560
steeped in the best traditions
of our great nation.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.100
If you don't lead by
example, who will?

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.830
LARRY ARNN: The College was
founded for four things--

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:52.310
freedom, faith,
learning, and character.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.670
If you read the 1844 document
in beautiful language,

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:56.540
that's what it says.

00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:02.330
SPEAKER: Your education
in the liberal arts

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.780
has empowered you to conserve
the foundations of our freedom.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.670
And you are now
uniquely suited, and I

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.000
believe you are uniquely
called to renew the fabric

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.580
of our national life.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.930
It will require courage
and tenacity and greatness

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:16.470
of spirit.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.320
At times, you will
face opposition.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.620
But as you've learned
here at Hillsdale,

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.098
strength rejoices
in the challenge.

00:01:28.098 --> 00:01:30.640
LARRY ARNN: The term "education"
comes from a Latin term that

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.370
means to "lead forth," educare.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.590
And "forth" is the
interesting part, right?

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.720
Because you have to
know which way is forth.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.060
And forth is a value term.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:40.840
There's back.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.655
And there's decline,
and there's decay,

00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:46.930
and there's forth and rising.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.640
The source of the crisis in
our country is in education.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.780
Some philosophers invented a
new way of looking at the world.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:57.310
And then this new
way, they thought,

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:02.680
we are actually not a thing with
a nature touched by the divine.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.550
Instead, what we are is
a kind of evolving thing.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:09.220
And that turned education
away from what it used to be,

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.920
to be an exercise to try to
control influences on us.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.470
Well, Thomas Jefferson
writes of the laws of nature

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:22.540
and of nature's God, it is the
way of things and knowing them,

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.960
and then being able to
see your part in things.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.700
And that makes us responsible
for ourselves in a bigger way.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.160
It takes energy to learn.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.660
It takes focus.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.212
You have to give yourself to it.

00:02:37.212 --> 00:02:40.474
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:42.250
JOSH: My name is Josh.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:43.750
I'm from Pella, Iowa.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.710
RYAN: I'm Ryan from
Temecula, California.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:47.710
NATE: My name is Nathan.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.420
I'm from Oshkosh, Wisconsin.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.240
RITA: My name is Rita, and
I am from Nairobi, Kenya.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.130
CALEB: My name is Caleb, and
I'm from Holland, Michigan.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:58.820
CLARA: My name is Clara,
and I'm from Cincinnati.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.370
I was always planning
to go to music school.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.160
Coming to Hillsdale was
kind of a big shift for me.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.410
CALEB: I was recruited by a
lot of Division II schools

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:07.580
to go and play football.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.182
A lot of them felt
the same, but when

00:03:09.182 --> 00:03:10.640
I stepped foot on
Hillsdale, I just

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.200
knew it was a different place.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.090
RYAN: My sister and
I were home-schooled.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:17.400
Some people always ask me,
whoa, do you have friends?

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.190
How did you socialize?

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.320
RITA: When I first decided
to come to Hillsdale,

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.340
my parents and I had to
make some hard choices.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.660
Leaving the country
as a 20-year-old

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.730
was a hard thing to do.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.230
And (CHOKING UP) I really
respected my parents

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:34.830
in letting me do this.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:35.330
Sorry.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.500
I'm kind of tearing up.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.340
[CHUCKLES]

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.860
NATE: When I had made the
decision to come to Hillsdale,

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.860
I knew nothing about what
the liberal arts were.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:43.735
JOSH: It was hard for
me to first understand,

00:03:43.735 --> 00:03:44.900
how is this practical?

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.320
How is this useful?

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.110
RYAN: Everything in
life is intertwined,

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.825
and that's what the liberal
arts experience captures.

00:03:50.825 --> 00:03:52.700
CLARA: Just the idea
that it takes a lot more

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.980
than one discipline to make you
into a well-educated person.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.880
NATE: It doesn't just
seek to develop skills

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.160
in a specific area,
but to develop

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.180
the character of the individual
and the ability to learn.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.130
DAVID M. WHALEN: What a
human being has to do,

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:09.350
strange as it
sounds, a human being

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.200
has to be taught
to see the world.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.750
We don't actually see reality
very clearly if we're just

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.410
left to our own devices.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.560
We entertain all kinds
of wishful thinking.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.390
A liberal education
liberates people

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:24.020
from all those constraints on
their understanding reality.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.780
DIANE PHILLIPP: It's a time
when college-aged students are

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.760
really making more
choices probably

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.690
than any other
time in their life.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.520
We have to help them understand
how to reach towards the good.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.040
Those are the kind of people
that other people follow.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:38.930
Those are leaders.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:42.230
LARRY ARNN: It's very hard
to take an 18-year-old

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.470
and help them become an
educated 21-year-old adult.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.240
It's a pain on
everyone involved.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:48.024
[CHUCKLES]

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:49.910
It's also great.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:52.880
And the point is
you orient yourself

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.690
around a combination of
surveying the necessary things

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:59.350
and digging deeply
into the best things.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:01.910
But you got to read philosophy
and rhetoric and logic

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.610
and economics, politics,
and history, and literature.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.110
And you've got to study
math, chemistry, biology,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:07.700
and physics.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.120
And so that means that
you're addressing yourselves

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.780
to the great subjects that
make up human knowledge.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.690
And there, that's
an achievement,

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:22.130
to be a person when somebody
says, what do you need to know?

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.170
Wouldn't it be great to
have an answer to that?

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:25.310
And one can have an answer.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.490
Hillsdale College was founded
on the frontier in 1844,

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.430
and it was founded by a bunch
of Free Will Baptist preachers

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.130
who came out here on the
frontier to start a college.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:41.460
DAVID M. WHALEN: They were very
serious about high learning

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.710
and about the
perpetuation of freedom,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.350
and very much founded the
school from its beginning

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.930
to admit people of
any race, both sexes.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.390
So it was pretty radical.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:54.085
LARRY ARNN: And they rode
their horse into various towns,

00:05:54.085 --> 00:05:56.460
and the one who came here is
a man named Ransom Dunn, who

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:57.930
worked here for 50 years.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.370
Ransom Dunn rode into
Hillsdale, and he said,

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.460
I'd like to meet with
the citizens interested

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:03.760
in education.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.640
And so they met
in the city hall,

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.480
but it didn't have anything
to do with government.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.030
Interesting point,
to enter the college,

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:13.540
you had to read Latin and Greek.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.380
And if you didn't
read those things,

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.450
they started a prep school
where you could just

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:21.080
learn those things, and then
you could come to college.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:22.400
Why?

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.830
They're going to read the
old books, the classic books,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:27.800
the first books written in
the West, of philosophy.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.570
And the Bible, the
New Testament's

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:32.810
in Greek, and so it's
intellectually very ambitious.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:34.550
This is going to be
a serious college.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:36.260
This is going to be
a Christian college.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.970
This is going to be
a freedom college.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.658
This college is not
going like slavery.

00:06:40.658 --> 00:06:42.950
DAVID M. WHALEN: So much so,
that during the Civil War,

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:44.750
the College almost closed.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:47.390
So many students up
and joined the Army.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:50.420
The College was kind of a
ghost college for a while.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:53.450
There were just very
few students left.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.910
Of course, they realized
that these students are just

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.730
doing exactly what we
believe is most important,

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:00.830
and that is defending
the country,

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.810
defending the unity
of the country,

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:07.160
and looking to end slavery,
this terrible moral blot

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:07.820
on the country.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:14.080
The College then, after
the war, when most colleges

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.630
and universities in the
country were taken up

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:19.820
in the sort of heady days
of educational reform,

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.320
Hillsdale never lost sight
of its founding principles,

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:26.350
and thought, we don't
actually have the liberty

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.730
to cashier our principles
and rewrite the nature

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.600
and mission of the institution.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.600
And then, of course,
in the 1950s,

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.240
the federal government started
funding higher education.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:41.470
And Hillsdale College was one of
the very few perhaps eccentric

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:43.310
institutions that
said, nope, nope, nope,

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:44.500
we're not going to do it.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:49.270
Hillsdale College understands
its independence is essentially

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.730
preserved by not
becoming a branch

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.230
of the federal government.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:54.820
LARRY ARNN: The
biggest detriment

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.920
of not taking money is one
doesn't have the money.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.500
[CHUCKLES] The
biggest benefit is it

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.460
reminds you what you're for,
and we focused on our mission

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.240
around here.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:05.740
The people who
support the College--

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:06.910
just think about that--

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.890
they don't get anything for it.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:14.210
They support the College because
they love the kind of thing

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:14.710
it is.

00:08:17.773 --> 00:08:19.190
DAVID M. WHALEN:
Hillsdale College

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:20.870
was founded as a
Christian college.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:22.760
It remains a Christian
college, of course.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.420
There is some tendency to think
that a Christian college means

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:30.410
a college that has foreclosed
important questions

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:33.860
and is really a kind of
indoctrination institution.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:37.429
Quite the reverse, it
opens the education up

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:40.220
to the ultimate point
at which everything

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:44.275
comes to be fully
meaningful and integrated.

00:08:44.275 --> 00:08:46.400
LARRY ARNN: You can't have
a liberal arts education

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.080
without God, because
you would have

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.810
to do without the
idea of the perfect.

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.710
And sure enough, by logic,
that would deprive you

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:59.260
of understanding why anything
is higher than anything else.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:02.300
And so come to find
out the idea of God

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:07.760
is the motive toward
which all learning moves.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.300
Our college motto is "Strength
rejoices in the challenge."

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.900
And what does that mean?

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:16.420
It means it's a
rigorous undertaking

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:20.197
to try to know the best things
and live according to them.

00:09:20.197 --> 00:09:22.280
DIANE PHILLIPP: Some people
smile about our model,

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.390
that "Strength rejoices in the
challenge," but it's so true.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:28.490
Life is a challenge,
and if they don't

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.770
go through challenging
times here,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:33.133
they're not going to
be prepared for life.

00:09:33.133 --> 00:09:34.800
NATE: The value that
was instilled in me

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.090
the most by my family
was the hard work ethic.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.480
RITA: I grew up in a Christian
family, strong in faith

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.860
and strong as people.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.033
CALEB: My parents instilled in
me a very strong work ethic.

00:09:47.033 --> 00:09:48.450
They taught me
anything's possible

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:49.860
if you're willing to go get it.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.530
JOSH: "Strength rejoices in
the challenge" is in many ways

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.420
a battle cry for Hillsdale.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.730
RYAN: There are a lot of
times when the motto "Strength

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:59.080
rejoices in the challenge"
confuses me, because I'm

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.330
definitely not feeling strong.

00:10:00.330 --> 00:10:01.162
[CHUCKLES]

00:10:01.162 --> 00:10:02.620
CLARA: Freshman
and sophomore year,

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.530
maybe I did not rejoice
in the challenge

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:06.610
quite as much as I have
the last couple of years.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:07.170
[LAUGHING]

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.510
RYAN: I'm feeling like there
are three papers to do,

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.310
several books to read,
and I don't know how

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.600
I'm going to get it all done.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.090
JOSH: As someone preparing
himself to be a Marine officer,

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.370
this war cry of "Strength
rejoices in the challenge"

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:18.330
is so important.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.190
The places that are
the most uncomfortable

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.290
are sometimes the best
places to be because of how

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.060
they challenge and grow you.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.920
CLARA: There is just
an accomplishment

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.390
that you feel that
you don't feel

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:28.800
when things aren't as hard.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.330
RITA: Just coming
from a place that

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:32.660
trains you to embrace
these challenges,

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:36.540
it's a skill that you'll use
every single day of your life.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.700
CALEB: Whether it be
in the academic circle

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.760
where I didn't quite get the
grade I wanted on a paper,

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:44.220
or whether it be in sports,
where I thought I would come in

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.390
and be a starter one day.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.600
But I've seen myself
more in a backup role.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.660
You can see those
things as failures,

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.300
or you can see them as
opportunities to get better.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:56.310
RYAN: You're not going
to get strong in a day.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.240
But eventually, if you
keep working at it,

00:10:58.240 --> 00:10:59.573
you're going to reach your goal.

00:10:59.573 --> 00:11:01.530
JOSH: It requires to
embrace the challenges,

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.930
to embrace the suck, as
the Marine Corps would say.

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.720
NATE: Hillsdale College
is a very demanding place.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.927
There are numerous amount
of challenges that we face,

00:11:09.927 --> 00:11:11.760
and the hardest probably
being the balancing

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.170
of a lot of good things.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:14.940
Through that process,
you fail a lot,

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:16.530
but you learn
through that failure.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:19.680
RYAN: The purpose of "Strength
rejoices in the challenge"

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.190
is to build you into a better
person by the time you leave.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.540
[MUSIC PLAYING]

